@brief
    INC-77  Paged at 02:14. One order pinned a consumer for seventy
            seconds and filled Sentry with the same stack trace
            twenty-five times. Refunds for everyone else were delayed
            behind it. Nothing is on fire now; the order is still
            unrefunded.

The log is the first buffer. Read it before you touch anything. It
contains the cause, the file and the line.

What you will find: the payment gateway answers `200` with a null refund
when it does not know the order. We read `.amount` off that null, get a
`TypeError`, and the consumer treats a `TypeError` like any other failure
and retries it twenty-five times. A `TypeError` will never succeed on the
second attempt.

Two fixes and a registration:

  1. payment.service.ts — a null refund is a known failure, not a crash.
     Directly under the existing status guard, separated by a blank line
     and in the same shape, throw `REFUND_ORDER_UNKNOWN` when
     `!response.data.refund`.

  2. refund.consumer.ts — do not retry that one. The retry condition
     becomes `error.code !== 'REFUND_ORDER_UNKNOWN' && message.attempt < 25`.

  3. errors/default.json — register `REFUND_ORDER_UNKNOWN` directly under
     `ORDER_NOT_FOUND`. It is a 404 and it is not our bug, so it does not
     go to Sentry.

The log and order.provider.ts are evidence. Neither changes.

@keys
| `4Y` `3j` `p` | copy a guard with its blank line and put it below |
| `ci(` | change the whole condition inside the brackets |
| `ci'` `ci"` | change inside the quotes |
| `:bn` | next buffer |

@hints
Start in the log and read down. The interesting lines are the gateway
response body, the stack frame with the file and line number in it, and
the attempt counter climbing.
--
Once you know the cause, all three edits are small. The new guard in the
service is the guard above it with a different condition and a different
code, so copy it. Yank four lines — the guard and the blank line — so
the spacing comes with it.
--
In the service: `/response.status !== 200` `Enter`, `4Y` `3j` `p`, then
`f(` `ci(!response.data.refund` `Esc` and `j0f'` `ci'REFUND_ORDER_UNKNOWN`
`Esc`. In the consumer the whole change is one `ci(` on the retry
condition. In the JSON it is `yyp` and a `ci"`.

@solution
A route that measures 225 keystrokes once you have read the log. Par
leaves room for the reading.

**payment.service.ts** — `:bn`, `/response.status !== 200` `Enter`,
`4Y` `3j` `p`, `f(` `ci(!response.data.refund` `Esc`,
`j0f'` `ci'REFUND_ORDER_UNKNOWN` `Esc`.

**refund.consumer.ts** — `:bn`, `/attempt < 25` `Enter`,
`ci(error.code !== 'REFUND_ORDER_UNKNOWN' && message.attempt < 25` `Esc`.

**errors/default.json** — `:bn`, `/ORDER_NOT_FOUND` `Enter`, `yyp`,
`ci"REFUND_ORDER_UNKNOWN` `Esc`. Then `:wqa`.

The editing here is easy. What the lesson is actually about is the two
minutes before it.

**The log is a file, so read it like one.** `/error` `Enter` and `n` walk
the failures. `/attempt=` shows you the retry counter climbing, which is
what tells you the loop is the second bug rather than a symptom of the
first. `G` gets you to the end, where the consumer gives up. None of this
needs a log viewer, and searching a log in the same editor as the code
means the file and line in a stack frame are two keystrokes from being
open.

**Fix both bugs.** The instinct under pressure is to fix the crash and
close the incident. The crash cost one order. The retry loop cost every
other customer in the queue seventy seconds, and it will do it again for
the next unrecoverable error, whatever that turns out to be. An incident
almost always has a failure and an amplifier, and the amplifier is the
one that wakes you up.

**A new failure mode needs a name.** `REFUND_ORDER_UNKNOWN` in the error
file is what makes the consumer's condition readable and what stops the
next person adding a second special case by hand. Registering the code
is not paperwork; it is the part of the fix the next incident depends on.

